what if you could relive moments of  someone else's life  see what they see touch what they touch  and feel what they feel  all from the comfort of your own home in  cyberpunk 2077  this kind of tech exists and it's called  brain dance  braindance or bd for short is the most  popular form of entertainment in the  cyberpunk universe  although braindance shares some minor  similarities with vr headsets the  technology couldn't be more different  unlike virtual reality bd can engage all  five senses by stimulating your brain in  a variety of ways  this results in a fully immersive  experience that lets you live out  scripted moments as someone else  well the most obvious use for this tech  is porn  it was originally used to rehabilitate  convicted criminals  money now or i will drop your sword  again  invented in 2007 by a uc santa cruz  student named yuriko tsujimoto shout out  to the banana slugs watching  bd was originally called the alternative  reality  process using a netrunner interface  tsujimoto was able to record her  thoughts  feelings and emotions onto a memory chip  and replay them using a modified cyber  deck after tsujimoto finished up her phd  she went to work for the state of  california as a researcher for penal  reform  with the help of a renowned psychologist  named norman lassemer tsujimoto began  developing ways the alternative reality  process  could reprogram and rehabilitate  criminals  using a rudimentary form of bd prisoners  were forced to relive their crimes over  and over again with exaggerated  consequences and  sometimes with incredibly painful  outcomes  [Music]  although many considered it torture it  was effective  however the alternative reality process  faced one major setback that prevented  wider use  most criminals weren't being recorded  when they committed a crime this made it  difficult for a prisoner to receive a  braindance treatment that matched the  offense  to rectify this tsujimoto and lassemer  received funding from both the state of  california and militech to reenact  crimes using  incarcerated volunteers however  recording the crime was only half of it  they also needed to record the  punishment to really hammer home the  whole  rehabilitation side of the experience as  lawless as the cyberpunk universe may be  torturing people to record their pain  was a step too far  or at least it was until a convicted  mass murder by the name of harold d  mcleroy made an interesting offer to  militech militech could record his death  if they generously compensated his  family  militech paid up and put him to death  before the ink on his contract dried  after mick leroy's deal other inmates  proposed similar offers and tsujimoto  began to expand her death and torture  database  thanks to tsujimoto's tech prisoners  were getting rehabilitated at a much  quicker rate than usual  thus freeing up space in prisons as  you'd expect  this method of rehabilitation isn't the  most ethical  many convicts walked away with a litany  of anxiety disorders  with the success of the altered reality  process on inmates tsujimoto and  lassemer formed their own company called  braindance incorporated  shortly after militech partnered with  braindance inc to create military  simulations for training purposes  you know just like that black mirror  episode it can feed you everything that  you did  you will see and smell  and feel it all in 2077 brain dance is  still used for criminal rehabilitation  and military training but it's primarily  used for entertainment  pretty much anyone and everyone  regardless of their socioeconomic  background has  access to a brain dance machine of  course the wealthy can afford top of the  line personal machines but bt arcades  can be found all throughout night city  inside customers can choose from a wide  selection of cartridges to rent and test  out in the arcade itself  in order to experience braindance you  need a bd headset  also known as a wreath and a base  station also known as a feeder  once the user is connected to the unit  it will force the user into a  trance-like state making them  susceptible to other sensory experiences  in the early days of consumer bd most  experiences put the user in the shoes of  someone else doing something  extraordinary  a famous athlete making a game-winning  goal climbing mount everest or reliving  a significant historical event  as the bd industry grew production  companies began creating more in-depth  curated experiences  by 2077 bd covers a wide range of genres  and tastes both  legal and illegal of course there's also  a bunch of weird sex bd cartridges like  sasha devon's three mouths one desire  but there are also horror experiences  like foreign body and popular slice of  life beaties that let you inhabit the  body of a celebrity  kind of like the 1999 comedy being john  malkovich  malkovich there are even some religious  bds that let the user experience jesus's  suffering on the cross first hand which  sounds like a big old yikes to me but  considering people knowingly went to see  passion of the christ in droves i can  see why that might be popular  it's also important to note that  braindance is heavily edited  very rarely is the user given a raw bd  recording to playback because it can be  very difficult to seamlessly plant the  user in someone else's mind  there would be too many irrelevant  thoughts that require context and  memories that are incomprehensible to a  foreign entity in order to clean up a bd  recording you need an editor to pour  over each scene and remove thoughts  emotions and sensations that would be  unrelated to the user  basically if you're making an action bd  you don't want the user to think about  what the actor ate for breakfast  after the bd recording has been edited  the editor may remix the active's  feelings and emotions to give more  weight to certain moments  for instance if the user should be  scared the editor might inject a little  bit more fear into that recording  kind of like how movies attempt to  subconsciously manipulate someone's  feelings with different camera angles  and focal lengths  you see in this world there's two kinds  of people my friend  those with loaded guns  you dig without a certain level of  editing braindance can be very  off-putting and even dangerous  despite the dangers though there is  still a market for more extreme and  authentic bd  that kind of bd goes by the name black  brain dance black brain dance is hard to  come by and usually features recordings  of criminal activity  extreme violence or snuff unlike the  regulated bd you might find an arcade or  a store  black brain dance can be very dangerous  to the user due to the unedited extreme  emotions it can impose  while over-the-counter brain dance isn't  dangerous per se  it can lead to some negative side  effects the obvious one is addiction  many bd setups include a feeder which  gives the user sustenance so they can  stay in a bd transfer as long as  possible  as you can imagine many folks end up  preferring life in a braindance trance  over reality  those who overuse bd are often rendered  numb and emotionless because reality  doesn't quite stir up the same intense  emotions as a well-produced bd  it can also lead to bddid which stands  for braindance dissociative identity  disorder  basically the user spends so much time  in someone else's head that they are  unable to distinguish the self from the  role they take in a bd  this usually happens to those who  routinely return to bds starring their  favorite actors  of course if you saw the first night  city wire you got a sneak peek at how  braindance works in game it will be  interesting to see how much depth  braindance will have when cyberpunk  comes out this november  if you're interested in learning more  about braindance and cyberpunk lore i  highly recommend picking up the  cyberpunk  2077 world book a lot of the info in  this video comes from that very book  but in the meantime for more cyberpunk  lore be sure to check back every  saturday for another video if there's a  topic you'd like to see covered please  let us know in the comments below and  with that  we'll see you next time  you
 

